# Biomolecules‐Incorporated Metal‐Organic Frameworks Gated Light‐Sensitive Organic Photoelectrochemical Transistor for Biodetection

## abstract
Incorporating biomolecules into metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs) as exoskeletons to form biomolecules‐MOFs biohybrids has attracted great attention as an emerging class of advanced materials. Organic devices have been shown as powerful platforms for next‐generation bioelectronics, such as wearable biosensors, tissue engineering constructs, and neural interfaces. Herein, biomolecules‐incorporated MOFs as innovative gating module is realized for the first time, which is exemplified by biocatalytic precipitation (BCP)‐oriented horseradish peroxidase (HRP)‐embedded zeolitic imidazolate framework‐90 (HRP@ZIF‐90)/CdIn2S4 gated organic photoelectrochemical transistor under light illumination. In connection to a sandwich immunocomplexing targeting the model analyte human IgG, the IgG‐dependent generation of H2O2 and the tandem HRP‐triggered BCP reaction can cause the in situ blocking of the pore network of ZIF‐90, leading to variant gating effect with corresponding responses of the device. This representative biodetection achieved good analytical performance with a wide linear range and a low detection limit of 100 fg mL−1. In the view of the plentiful biomolecule‐MOF complexes and their potential interactions with organic systems, this study provides a proof‐of‐concept study for the generic development of biomolecules‐MOFs‐gated electronics and beyond.

Incorporating biomolecules into metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as exoskeletons to form biomolecules-MOFs biohybrids has attracted great attention as an emerging class of advanced materials. Organic devices have been shown as powerful platforms for next-generation bioelectronics, such as wearable biosensors, tissue engineering constructs, and neural interfaces. Herein, biomolecules-incorporated MOFs as innovative gating module is realized for the first time, which is exemplified by biocatalytic precipitation (BCP)-oriented horseradish peroxidase (HRP)-embedded zeolitic imidazolate framework-90 $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathsf{H R P}@\mathsf{Z I F}\cdot\mathsf{S0})/\mathsf{C d}\mathsf{I n}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}}\end{array}$ gated organic photoelectrochemical transistor under light illumination. In connection to a sandwich immunocomplexing targeting the model analyte human IgG, the IgG-dependent generation of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ and the tandem HRP-triggered BCP reaction can cause the in situ blocking of the pore network of ZIF-90, leading to variant gating effect with corresponding responses of the device. This representative biodetection achieved good analytical performance with a wide linear range and a low detection limit of 100 fg $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ . In the view of the plentiful biomolecule-MOF complexes and their potential interactions with organic systems, this study provides a proof-of-concept study for the generic development of biomolecules-MOFs-gated electronics and beyond.
## 1. Introduction
Rational integration between the organic electrochemical transistor $\mathrm{(OECT)^{[1-6]}}$ and photoelectrochemistry $(\mathrm{PEC})^{[7-11]}$ taking place in organic optoelectronics leads to the advanced light-sensitive organic photoelectrochemical transistor (OPECT), which has been shown to be a versatile platform to study the rich light-material-bio interaction and to develop new light-sensitive biosensors,[12–16] bioelectronics[17] and optologics.[18] In such devices, semiconducting interfacial properties of light-harvesting gates have been proven to be of intimate relevance to the potentiometric performance of devices, while the high accessibility of the semiconductors provides the possibility for biological interfacing. In addition to reduced power consumption, light-induced voltage could potentially save one power supply for gate voltage $(V_{\mathrm{G}})$ , which is a disadvantage to the stability of both the device in an aqueous electrolyte and fragile biomolecules such as proteins and enzymes.[19] Removal of $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ is even desirable as it could make the system structurally simplified in case of strict spatial limitations when implemented in bioelectronic devices.[20] Despite its apparent advantages, the study in OPECT is still in its early stage and much remains to be learned and done.
The ability to biologically regulate materials is a long-standing practice in chemistry and electronics that has led to advances in the development of various biodevices.[21–23] Innovative materials with external stimuli-responses lay the foundation for this end.[24] Composed of organic ligands and metal ions/clusters, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) is an interesting family of porous crystalline materials with high porosity, high specific surface area, adjustable structures and etc.[25–27] Recent reports showed the viability of biomolecules encapsulated within MOFs, and the porous MOF exoskeletons could serve as the protective cages for the fragile biomolecules and permit the diffusion of guest species via their pore network,[28–30] allowing emerging applications in biosensing,[31] biocatalysis,[32] and cancer therapy.[33,34] Despite the existing advances, no effort has been made to explore the gating properties of biomolecules-incorporated MOFs. We thus expected the rational utilization of such biohybrids for regulating innovative electronics.
Here, as shown in Figure  1, we present a proof-of-concept system that is exemplified by horseradish peroxidase (HRP)- embedded zeolitic imidazolate framework-90 $(\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF-90})/$ $\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/$ fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO)-gated poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) poly(styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS) OPECT system, which exhibits sensitive response to external $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ stimulus with corresponding fine gating properties. In connection to a sandwich immunorecognition composed of human immunoglobulin G (HIgG) as the model target antigen $({\mathrm{Ag}})$ , its primary capture antibody $(\mathrm{Ab}_{1})$ and glucose oxidase (GOx)/gold nanoparticles (Au NPs) labeled secondary antibody $(\mathrm{Ab}_{2})$ , biocatalytic production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the tandem HRP-triggered biocatalytic precipitation (BCP) process can cause the in situ blocking of the pore network of the biohybrid (Figure 1a), leading to the drastically altered interfacial charge/ mass transfer resistance with amplified transistor responses $(I_{\mathrm{DS}})$ (Figure  1b). This representative system achieved good analytical performance in terms of wide linear range and high sensitivity. This work featured the elegant utilization of biomolecules-incorporated MOF for innovative OPECT modulation and application. Due to the diversity of biomolecules-MOFs composites, this platform could function as a common basis for advanced MOFs-gated electronic biodevices.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Materials Fabrication and Analysis
To verify the gating properties of biomolecules-incorporated MOFs, we first prepared ZIF-90 and HRP $@$ ZIF-90 by a coprecipitation method, and then their BCP-oriented catalytic activities were analyzed.[29] The ZIF-90 was chosen as the representative protective cage due to its advantages of mild synthesis conditions, good biocompatibility, high chemical stability, and small pore size that could prevent the leaching of encapsulated proteins.[35,36] As shown in Figure 2a, the color of pristine ZIF-90 (I) suspension was light-yellow, while the one of free HRP (II) was a colorless solution. After HRP encapsulation, the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ (III) exhibited a similar light-yellow color. Subsequently, the catalytic activities of these three samples toward $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were investigated. The color of ZIF-90 suspension (IV) did not change, indicating that it had no peroxidase-like catalytic activity. However, the colors of free HRP and HRP $@$ ZIF-90 solutions changed significantly to blue (V) and blue-green (VI), respectively, indicating the well-retained HRP viability in HRP $@$ ZIF-90. Incidentally, the chemical stability of $\mathrm{HRP}@$ ZIF-90 was studied upon extreme conditions as displayed in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). Compared with pure HRP, the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ maintained higher catalytic activity toward TMB after treatment in $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide or at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ indicating that the ZIF-90 skeleton can protect the HRP and improve its chemical stability.
The capability of BCP for blocking the pores of biohybrid was studied by enzymatically converting soluble 4-chloro1-naphthol (4-CN) to insoluble 4-chloro-1-naphthol (4-CD) precipitation in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .[37,38] As shown, the original light-yellow $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF-}90$ suspension (III) was transformed into purple $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF-90@BCP}$ suspension (VII) due to the production of precipitation. Further treatment with TMB could not induce obvious change of the color (VIII) that was confirmed by the UV–vis absorption spectrum (Figure S2,
Supporting Information). This phenomenon indicated that the pores of ZIF-90 could be efficiently blocked by BCP. Before the BCP reaction, the pristine porous ZIF-90 allowed TMB to pass through its pores and be catalyzed by the embedded HRP, whereas the pores were blocked by precipitation to hinder the TMB-HRP interaction after the BCP reaction.
As shown in Figure  2b and inset, the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) revealed that the as-prepared ZIF-90 was of dodecahedronal morphology with a smooth surface. Figure  2c and inset show that the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ maintained the original morphology except that the surface was slightly roughened, which was further proved by transmission electron microscopy analysis (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The powder X-ray diffraction showed no significant difference in crystal structure and crystallinity between ZIF-90 and $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\cdot90$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The nitrogen adsorption and desorption isotherms revealed that $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ had a smaller total pore volume compared to pure ZIF-90 due to the embedded HRP molecules (Figure S5, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure S6a (Supporting Information), the corresponding Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy demonstrated the HRP was successfully incorporated within ZIF-90. This was also verified by the thermogravimetric analysis, as the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ sample showed a deeper drop above ${\approx}330~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which was attributed to the decomposition of the HRP in $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\cdot90$ (Figure S6b, Supporting Information). By contrast, after the BCP reaction, as displayed in Figure 2d and inset, the SEM images showed that many precipitate nanoparticles were adsorbed on the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\cdot90$ with an obvious rough surface, which further validated the successful BCP reaction enabled by HRP $@$ ZIF-90.
On the other hand, the photosensitive $\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ electrode was prepared by a hydrothermal method.[39] As shown in Figure  2e and inset, it exhibited the surface morphology of crossed nanoflakes and could serve well as a substrate for HRP $@$ ZIF-90 immobilization, which was verified by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Figure 2f, the XPS spectra showed characteristic peaks of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ , indicating that $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ was successfully modified on the $\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/$ FTO electrode.
Next, the biorelevant $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -triggered BCP reaction on the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}.90/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ electrode was verified by connecting to an immunosandwich assay $(\mathrm{Ab_{1}/H I g G/A b_{2}\cdot A u}$ $\mathrm{{NPs/GOx})}$ in a 96-well plate and characterized by a conventional PEC experiment.[40,41] Specifically, the HIgG-dependent $(100~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}})$ $\mathrm{GOx/Au\NPs{\cdot}A b_{2}}$ catalyzed glucose to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which triggered HRP-enabled BCP reaction to in situ block the pore network of ZIF-90. As displayed in Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ , under intermittent illumination, the bare $\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ electrode presented a large anodic photocurrent of ${\approx}2.7~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (black curve) with the electron donor of 0.01 m triethanolamine (TEOA), and the reproducible PEC responses of the $\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/$ FTO electrode within 20 light on–off cycles indicated its good photophysical stability (Figure S7, Supporting Information). After $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF-}90$ modification, the photocurrent decreased to ${\ \approx}1.6~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (red curve), which could be attributed to the reduced interfacial mass transfer efficiency. Remarkably, the photocurrent further drastically decreased to ${\approx}0.7~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ after the BCP reaction (blue curve), indicating that the BCP-induced blockage of the pores could severely inhibit the charge transfer between the TEOA and electrode (Figure S8, Supporting Information), which was also verified by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (Figure S9, Supporting Information).
## 2.2. Biomolecules-Incorporated MOFs-Gated OPECT
Generally, a typical OECT operates upon that a minute gate voltage change could drive ions in the electrolyte medium to dope/de-dope the organic semiconductor film, resulting in an amplified channel current $(I_{\mathrm{DS}})$ . Initially, the fabricated PEDOT:PSS channel was characterized using a traditional $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ gate and its transfer and output characteristics were recorded in Figure 3a,b, respectively. The decrease in $I_{\mathrm{DS}}$ upon the application of positive $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ clearly indicated the $V_{\mathrm{{G}}}.$ - dependent modulation behavior of the system. The stable overlapped transfer curves during long-term measurements of up to $360~\mathrm{min}$ demonstrated the high stability of the device. The transient response behavior of the device was investigated by switching the $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ from 0 to $0.3{\mathrm{~V}}.$ As recorded in Figure  3c, the $I_{\mathrm{DS}}$ responded rapidly to $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ with no obvious decay within ten cycles, which further indicated the sensitive $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ -dependent modulation and satisfactory stability of the device. Subsequently, the developed light-sensitive $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\mathrm{-}90/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ gate was combined with the PEDOT:PSS-based channel to construct the designed OPECT biosensor, which was first investigated by tracking the responses of $I_{\mathrm{GS}}$ and $\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{DS}}}$ upon the intermittent white light irradiation $(0.48\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ). As depicted in Figure 3d, upon illumination and $0\mathrm{V}$ of applied $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ , the $I_{\mathrm{GS}}$ displayed a tiny current step of ${\approx}420$ nA (red curve), whereas the $I_{\mathrm{DS}}$ exhibited a substantial current step of $\approx560~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (blue curve). This photoresponsive behavior could be attributed to the PEC modulation of the channel conductance via ions immigration driven by the light-induced photovoltage $(V_{\mathrm{P}})$ at the gate/electrolyte interface. As illustrated in Figure 3e, without illumination, the conductive oxidized $\mathrm{PEDOT^{+}}$ in the pristine self-doped PEDOT+: $\mathrm{PSS^{-}}$ film kept the channel in a high conductance state. However, under light irradiation, the generated positive $V_{\mathrm{P}}$ could drive the cations in the electrolyte to penetrate into the polymeric channel and electrochemically de-doped PEDOT+: $\mathrm{PSS^{-}}$ film, which resulted in the generation of PEDOT0 and thereby the low channel conductance. As indicated in Figure  3f, the corresponding gain of current was above $10^{3}$ , indicating that the current signal generated by the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\mathrm{-}90/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ electrode could be amplified by more than 3 orders of magnitude, which significantly surpassed the conventional PEC technique.[7]
Subsequently, the gating effect of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\cdot90$ on the proposed OPECT system was further investigated. As illustrated in Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ , without illumination, the two similar transfer curves (blue and red solid lines) were measured before and after the BCP reaction, while these two curves shifted toward the negative gate voltage (blue and red dashed lines) under illumination. To be specific, in the presence of light, the transfer curve of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}\mathrm{-}90/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ apparently moved to the negative $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ by ${\approx}620~\mathrm{mV}$ (calculated with reference to the $I_{\mathrm{DS}}$ of $-700\upmu\mathrm{A})$ , whereas after BCP reaction it shifted only by $\mathrm{\approx}160~\mathrm{mV},$ demonstrating the remarkable gating effect of the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF-}90$ against the developed OPECT device. This behavior was explained by the mechanism of interfacial potential distribution, as depicted in Figure 3h. Essentially, the ionic circuit of the device contains two capacitors in series, one capacitor $(C_{\mathrm{G-E}})$ corresponds to the electric double layer (EDL) at the gate/electrolyte interface and the other capacitor $(C_{\mathrm{C-E}})$ corresponds to the channel/electrolyte interface. Without illumination, for the initial $\mathrm{HRP}@Zmathrm{I F}{\cdot}90/$ $\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/$ FTO-based device, the potential drop at the channel/ electrolyte interface was $V_{\mathrm{C-E}}={\frac{V_{G}}{1+\gamma}}~(\gamma={\frac{C_{\mathrm{C-E}}}{C_{\mathrm{G-E}}}})$ CC E ). In the presence of illumination, the generation of positive $V_{\mathrm{P-I}}$ on the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ gate increased the effective gate voltage $(V_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{eff-I}})$ and thus $V_{\mathrm{C-E}}$ to $\frac{V_{\mathrm{G}}}{1+\gamma}+V_{\mathrm{P-I}}$ . The corresponding $V_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{eff-I}}$ of the device could be described by the following equation:[42]
$$
V_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{eff-I}}=V_{\mathrm{G}}+(1+\gamma)V_{\mathrm{P-I}}
$$
Since the increase of $V_{\mathrm{C-E}}$ enhanced the electrochemical de-doping of PEDOT:PSS channel by cations in the electrolyte, the transfer curve shifted toward the negative $V_{\mathrm{G}}$ . After the BCP reaction, the precipitation-induced pore blockage increased the interfacial charge transfer resistance and thus reduced the photoelectric conversion efficiency. Therefore, the BCP-treated electrode produced a lower $V_{\mathrm{P-II}}$ and thus a lower $V_{\mathrm{G}}^{\mathrm{eff-II}}$ , resulting in a small negative shift of the transfer curve. Figure  3i demonstrated the corresponding $\ensuremath{I_{\mathrm{DS}}}$ signals change of the devices before and after the BCP reaction. Here, the $\boldsymbol{I}_{\mathrm{DS-step}}$ was defined as the $\lvert I_{\mathrm{DS}}\rvert$ in darkness $\big(\big|I_{\mathrm{DS}}^{\mathrm{light}}\big|\big)$ minus the $\left\vert I_{\mathrm{DS}}\right\vert$ in light $\big(\big|I_{\mathrm{DS}}^{\mathrm{light}}\big|\big)$ . Upon illumination, the $\mathrm{HRP}@$ ZIF-90/CdIn2S4/FTO-based device produced a larger IDS-step of ${\approx}530~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (blue line). However, the presence of precipitates significantly reduced the $\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{DS-step}}}$ to ${\approx}180~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ (red line), which matched well with the variation in transfer characteristics curves.
## 2.3. Biodetection
Since the change of $\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{DS-step}}}$ associated closely with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ - induced BCP reaction on the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ gate, the proposed OPECT system was connected to the $\operatorname{GOx}/$ Au NPs-tagged sandwich immunoassay to achieve highly sensitive biodetection of HIgG as the model target. For better detection performance, the concentration of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ modified on the $\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{FTO}$ gate electrode was optimized (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Figure 4a displayed the $\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{DS-step}}}$ responses corresponding to different concentrations of HIgG and the response and recovery time was ${\approx}60$ s. As expected, the $I_{\mathrm{DS-step}}$ decreased with increasing HIgG concentration, demonstrating the enhancement of the immunerecognition-associated gating effect of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}90$ . Figure 4b showed good linearity from $1.0\ \mathrm{pg\mL^{-1}}$ to $100~\mathrm{{ng~mL^{-1}}}$ with a correlation coefficient of $R^{2}=0.991$ and a detection limit of 100 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $(S/\mathrm{N}>3)$ . We then investigated the selectivity of the system for some biological interferents, including rabbit IgG (RIgG), mouse IgG $({\mathrm{MIgG}})$ , cardiac troponin I (cTnI), carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA), and their mixed-sample. As shown in Figure 4c, despite the 10-fold greater concentration of the interferents, only HIgG and its mixture produced significant signal changes, indicating good selectivity of the biosensor. Compared with other methods for HIgG detection (Table S1), our method possesses a wider linear range and a relatively low detection limit, suggesting its good detection performance. The potential of this biosensor for real-world detection was investigated by adding HIgG to normal human serum. The serum samples were diluted to a specific concentration with 0.01 m PBS $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ solution, and the recovery rate of $1.0\ \mathrm{ng\mL^{-1}}$ HIgG was $93.5\%$ , indicating that our biosensor has acceptable accuracy for real-world application.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, based on the BCP-enabled pore blockage of HRP@ZIF-90, this work demonstrated the possibility of biomolecules-incorporated MOFs as an innovative gating module for light-sensitive OPECT operation and its practical biodetection application. Before BCP reaction, the porous HRP $@$ ZIF-90 permitted electron donors to pass through and transfer electrons to the photosensitive $\mathrm{CdIn_{2}S_{4}/F T O}$ electrode, whereas the blocked $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF-}90$ inhibited the mass diffusion and thus led to a distinct channel response after the BCP reaction. By coupling with a HIgG-targeted immunosandwich assay with GOx/Au NPs as the labels, the target-dependent catalytic generation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the tandem HRP-triggered BCP reaction could cause the in situ blocking of the pore network, thus underpinning the relationship between target concentration and the changes in $\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{DS}}}$ under light illumination. This biosensor achieved good analytical performance for HIgG with a wide linear range of $1.0\ \mathrm{pg\mL^{-1}}$ to $100\ \mathrm{ng\mL^{-1}}$ and a detection limit of $100~\mathrm{fg~mL^{-1}}$ . In light of the diversity of biomolecules-MOFs composites, this work is expected to stimulate more interest in exploring the potential of biomolecules-MOFs composites toward advanced bioelectronics.
